 "articles": [
  {
   "id": "T001",
   "title": "Carta de Jamaica",
   "aboutAuthor": "Simón Bolívar (1783–1830), born in Caracas, became a political and military leader of South American independence. After setbacks in Venezuela and New Granada, he went into exile in Kingston in 1815. He wrote before the campaigns and settlements that would establish Colombia, not as head of an already constructed continental bloc.",
   "workInItsTime": "The Jamaica Letter answers an English-speaking Caribbean interlocutor and explains Spanish America’s break to readers abroad. Bolívar combines diagnosis, prediction, and an effort to secure support. He distinguishes the desirability of American union from the geographic, social, and political obstacles to one government. Presenting the letter as an implemented plan erases the uncertainty that shap",
   "whyReadToday": "The letter lets us hear a revolutionary leader before the outcome looks inevitable. In diplomatic history it shows independence as a problem of international recognition and communication. Comparison with the Panama treaty moves from an exile’s hypotheses to commitments some governments were actually willing to sign."
  },
  {
   "id": "T013",
   "title": "Nuestra América",
   "aboutAuthor": "José Martí (1853–1895), born in Havana, experienced imprisonment and exile before organizing for Cuban independence from abroad. He lived and wrote in several Spanish American countries and in New York. Journalism taught him to observe United States institutions closely without losing sight of the danger its power might pose to its neighbors’ autonomy.",
   "workInItsTime": "Nuestra América appeared in 1891 amid arguments about American republics, hemispheric cooperation, and the balance of power with the United States. Martí rejects the mechanical importation of models and insists that government begin in knowledge of the continent’s own societies. He advocates neither racial homogeneity nor obedience to Spain, but the capacity of populations with histories and probl",
   "whyReadToday": "The essay gives projects of unity a demanding test: who understands the population and who can speak for it? Its continental scale does not erase the Cuban standpoint. Read beside Bello or Rodó, Martí helps us distinguish a common language, a program of cultural formation, and a politics grounded in local knowledge."
  },
  {
   "id": "T030",
   "title": "La raza cósmica — Ensayo «Misión de la raza iberoamericana»",
   "aboutAuthor": "José Vasconcelos (1882–1959), born in Oaxaca, was a writer, university rector, and secretary of public education in post-revolutionary Mexico. Educational and cultural campaigns gave institutional reach to ideas he also developed in essays. His positions changed over his lifetime; the argument of 1925 deserves to be situated in its own setting.",
   "workInItsTime": "The essay opening La raza cósmica argues that mixture could produce a new form of humanity and gives Ibero-America a historical role. Opposition to racial purity does not eliminate hierarchies, types, or judgments of selection from the text. The programmatic essay and the travel notes collected in some editions cover different ground. An edition giving only the opening essay should not be called t",
   "whyReadToday": "It is indispensable to understanding the appeal and limits of turning a history of mixture into a collective destiny. Read beside Ortiz, Bomfim, and Lezama, it distinguishes a prophecy about race from social or artistic accounts of cultural change. Readers learn more by exploring the contradictions than by endorsing or rejecting the book on its most famous slogan."
  }
 ]
